output:
{
  "title": "Before this is load-bearing: four measured constraints on the self-hosted deploy",
  "purpose": "Confirm the four operational constraints are understood and settled before this service carries anyone's work but mine. Answers become the deploy checklist entry in docs/SELF-HOSTING.md and decide the values that ship in the Deployment manifest and env — replicas, MAX_AWAIT_MS, LINK_TTL_HOURS — plus who owns link revocation on day one.",
  "resumeHint": "Once answers are in: set MAX_AWAIT_MS and LINK_TTL_HOURS in the deployment env, verify `replicas: 1` and `strategy: Recreate` in the Deployment, curl the edge for /mcp and /mcp.js to confirm cf-cache-status, then deploy.",
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Deploy owner",
      "role": "Ships the manifest and owns the values that go in it"
    },
    {
      "name": "Platform / networking",
      "role": "Knows what proxy or CDN sits in front and what it times out at"
    },
    {
      "name": "Team lead",
      "role": "Decides whether link-as-identity is acceptable for how the team will actually use this"
    }
  ],
  "visibility": "open",
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Read this before you deploy, not after",
        "lead": "Four constraints govern this service. None of them were anticipated in design — each was found in production, and each fails quietly rather than loudly. Three are configuration you must get right before the first real user; the fourth is a property of the product that no configuration will change.",
        "points": [
          "Exactly one replica. Replay protection does not degrade under horizontal scaling — it stops working.",
          "Long polls must return inside the proxy's ceiling. Past it, the caller cannot tell a dead wait from an empty one.",
          "A CDN may cache by file extension whatever your headers say. The failure is silent: everything works, one deploy behind.",
          "A participant link is a capability. Holding it means answering as that person. That is deliberate, and it is not an audit trail."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The four constraints, as measured",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "Run exactly one replica",
            "detail": "The replay-protection nonce store is in memory (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts). Every signed request carries a nonce accepted once, which is what stops a captured request being replayed inside its five-minute freshness window. With two replicas, a nonce burned on one is unknown to the other, and a replayed request routed to the second one succeeds. The Deployment therefore pins replicas: 1 with a Recreate strategy. Scaling out is possible — it means moving the nonce store to Postgres or Redis first. The moment replicas changes, that is the thing that has to change with it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Long polls die at about 100 seconds behind Cloudflare",
            "detail": "Cloudflare gives an origin roughly 100 seconds to respond before it answers 524 itself. Measured through the tunnel: a 150-second poll died at 125 seconds with HTTP 524 and no body. Not a slow response — no response. Holding a connection past that ceiling does not make the caller wait longer, it makes the wait fail. MAX_AWAIT_MS defaults to 90 seconds so every poll returns inside the window; one that runs out its budget comes back with timedOut: true and whatever has arrived so far. A timeout is a legitimate outcome, not an error. The MCP client loops shorter polls, so a caller asking for five minutes still gets five minutes."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A CDN may cache by file extension regardless of what you send",
            "detail": "/mcp.js was served with cache-control: no-store and came back from the edge with cf-cache-status: HIT and an injected max-age=14400. Cloudflare caches .js by default, by extension, whatever the origin intended. For four hours after every deploy, agents installed the previous client. The fix is in the path, not the headers: the canonical URL is /mcp, no extension. /mcp.js remains an alias for anyone who saved the old URL. Origin headers are a request, not an instruction."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Participant links are capabilities",
            "detail": "Anyone holding a participant link can answer as that participant. A forwarded link is a forwarded identity. This is why the product works at all: participants need no account, no email verification, and no Claude access. Attribution is whichever link was used, which costs nothing and is honest about what it is. It is not an audit trail — do not deploy this expecting one. If responses ever need to be contested, that is the point to add verified identity, and that is a change to this service, not a configuration option waiting to be switched on."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The settings these constraints pin down",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "replicas",
            "value": "1 — non-negotiable while the nonce store is in memory"
          },
          {
            "key": "Deployment strategy",
            "value": "Recreate — a rolling update would briefly run two replicas"
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce store",
            "value": "In memory, src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts. Move to Postgres or Redis before scaling out."
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce freshness window",
            "value": "5 minutes"
          },
          {
            "key": "MAX_AWAIT_MS",
            "value": "90000 by default — must sit below whatever the fronting proxy allows"
          },
          {
            "key": "Measured Cloudflare ceiling",
            "value": "~100s stated; a 150s poll died at 125s with HTTP 524, no body"
          },
          {
            "key": "Canonical MCP client URL",
            "value": "/mcp (no extension). /mcp.js is a legacy alias and is cacheable by extension."
          },
          {
            "key": "LINK_TTL_HOURS",
            "value": "Bounds how long a link stays answerable; expiry is checked in the token-resolving query, not after it"
          },
          {
            "key": "Revocation",
            "value": "revoked_at exists in the schema and is honoured on every resolve, but nothing exposes it over HTTP yet"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "Behind a proxy that is not Cloudflare, find its own timeout and set MAX_AWAIT_MS below it. Whatever sits in front of this service decides the real ceiling; the service only decides whether it respects it."
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "Killing a link today",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "There is no revocation endpoint. Until there is, revoking a single participant link is a direct update against participant_links. Confirm someone has database access and knows this before you hand out the first link.",
            "language": "sql",
            "code": "UPDATE participant_links\n   SET revoked_at = now()\n WHERE token = '<token>';"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "What I need settled before deploying"
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "Deploy checklist",
        "intro": "Answer from what you can actually verify, not what you expect to be true. A wrong answer here fails silently in production.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "boolean",
            "id": "replicas-pinned",
            "label": "Have you confirmed the Deployment pins replicas: 1 with a Recreate strategy?",
            "required": true,
            "help": "Check the manifest, and check that no autoscaler or platform default can raise it."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "fronting-proxy",
            "label": "What sits in front of this service, and what is its response timeout?",
            "required": true,
            "long": true,
            "help": "Name the proxy, CDN, ingress or load balancer, and the timeout you measured or found documented. If it is Cloudflare, say so."
          },
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "max-await-ms",
            "label": "What value should MAX_AWAIT_MS ship with?",
            "required": true,
            "min": 5000,
            "max": 300000,
            "help": "Milliseconds, comfortably below the ceiling above. 90000 if you are behind Cloudflare."
          },
          {
            "kind": "boolean",
            "id": "cdn-cache-checked",
            "label": "Has someone fetched /mcp through the edge and checked the cache status on the response?",
            "required": true,
            "help": "Look for cf-cache-status or the equivalent, plus any max-age the edge injected. A HIT on the client path means agents will install a stale client."
          },
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "link-ttl-hours",
            "label": "What should LINK_TTL_HOURS be?",
            "required": true,
            "min": 1,
            "help": "This is the only real control over how long a forwarded link stays usable. Shorter is safer; too short and people lose access mid-brief."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "revocation-owner",
            "label": "Who can revoke a link on the day it is needed, and how do they reach the database?",
            "required": true,
            "long": true,
            "help": "Name a person and a path to running the UPDATE above. 'We'll figure it out' is a valid answer only if you are willing to say it out loud."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "approval",
      "props": {
        "title": "Accepting links-as-identity",
        "proposal": "Deploy with participant links as the only form of identity, accepting that anyone holding a link can answer as that participant and that attribution is 'whichever link was used' rather than a verified identity.",
        "detail": "Approve only if no answer collected through this service will ever need to be contested — no approvals of record, no sign-offs anyone would later dispute, nothing that would be treated as evidence. If the team will use it for that, the honest answer is to reject and add verified identity first: it is a change to the service, not a setting. Rejecting does not block the deploy; it blocks the use case."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "The scaling question, answered now rather than at 2am",
        "context": "Single-replica is fine until it is not. The failure mode when someone raises replicas without moving the nonce store is not degraded performance — it is replay protection silently ceasing to exist. Decide the plan while nobody is under pressure.",
        "question": "What is the plan for when one replica is no longer enough?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "accept-single",
            "label": "Accept single-replica permanently for this use; the load will never justify more"
          },
          {
            "value": "guardrail-first",
            "label": "Stay single-replica, but add a guardrail now — a startup check or manifest comment tying replicas to the nonce store"
          },
          {
            "value": "port-nonces-now",
            "label": "Move the nonce store to Postgres or Redis before this goes load-bearing"
          },
          {
            "value": "defer-with-owner",
            "label": "Defer, with a named owner and a trigger condition written down"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
